// Functions considered, alphabetically, but deliberately left out of
// FunctionName/FUNCTION_SPECS -- a formula calling one of these just fails
// to parse (same as any other unrecognized function) rather than getting a
// unit transformer or inferencer:
//   ADDRESS -- builds a cell reference as text from row/column numbers
//     (and an optional sheet name); those numeric arguments identify a
//     position, not a unit-bearing quantity, so there's nothing for a unit
//     transformer to compute and no meaningful annotation to infer.
//   AGGREGATE -- its behavior (and argument shape) is chosen by a function
//     number in its first argument (SUM, AVERAGE, MIN, LARGE, PERCENTILE,
//     ...), so a single fixed transformer/inferencer can't cover it. Left
//     unsupported for now; if there's demand, a transformer/inferencer that
//     dispatches on that first argument's value -- delegating to the
//     transformer/inferencer already defined for the corresponding function,
//     where one exists -- would be the way to add it.
//   AND -- a logical function: its arguments are conditions (often
//     comparisons) and its result is a boolean, neither of which carries a
//     unit of measure, so there's nothing for a unit transformer or
//     inferencer to do.
//   ARABIC -- converts a Roman numeral text string to its Arabic-numeral
//     value; its argument is text, not a unit-bearing quantity, so there's
//     nothing for a unit transformer or inferencer to do.
//   AREAS -- counts the number of contiguous ranges in a reference; its
//     argument is a reference, not a unit-bearing value, and its result is
//     a plain count, so there's nothing for a unit transformer or
//     inferencer to do.
//   ARRAYTOTEXT -- renders an array's values as a single text string; its
//     result is text, not a unit-bearing quantity, so there's nothing for a
//     unit transformer or inferencer to do.
//   ASC -- converts full-width (double-byte) characters to half-width
//     (single-byte) characters in a text string; its argument and result
//     are both text, not unit-bearing quantities, so there's nothing for a
//     unit transformer or inferencer to do.
//   BAHTTEXT -- renders a number as Thai text with a baht currency suffix;
//     its result is text, not a unit-bearing quantity, so there's nothing
//     for a unit transformer or inferencer to do.
//   CALL -- invokes a function in an external DLL or code resource; what
//     units (if any) its arguments or result carry depends entirely on
//     which external function is being called, so there's no fixed
//     behavior a transformer or inferencer could catalog here.
//   CELL -- returns one of many different kinds of information (address,
//     format, width, protection status, ...) about a cell, chosen by its
//     first argument; the result's shape varies too widely (text, a plain
